 "prompt": "You are answering a biomedical research question.\n\nQuestion: Do inhaled corticosteroids affect perception of dyspnea during bronchoconstriction in asthma?\n\nAnswer \"yes\", \"no\", or \"maybe\", and give your confidence from 0 to 100 that your answer is correct.\n\nRespond with only a JSON object and no other text, in exactly this format:\n{\"answer\": \"yes\" or \"no\" or \"maybe\", \"confidence\": integer from 0 to 100}",
 "session_persisted": false,
 "text": "\n\n<｜DSML｜function_calls\n\n<｜DSML｜function_callsBased on my medical knowledge:\n\nInhaled corticosteroids (ICS) primarily work as anti-inflammatory agents to reduce airway inflammation in asthma. However, their effect on dyspnea perception during bronchoconstriction is complex. While ICS reduce airway inflammation and hyperresponsiveness, they don't directly affect the neural pathways involved in dyspnea perception. Some studies suggest ICS may modestly improve dyspnea perception by reducing airway inflammation, but the effect is not as pronounced as with bronchodilators. There is some evidence for a possible effect, but the literature is not conclusive.\n\n{\"answer\": \"maybe\", \"confidence\": 65}",
